ficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2152e47f-a35d-573c-ac57-9da1fd0fa273" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, $\\frac{{2}^{-4}}{{2}^{2}}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To divide powers of the same base, we simply subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{-4}}{{2}^{2}}={2}^{-4-2}={2}^{-6}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${2}^{-6},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $(2)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{2}^{-6}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{64}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "097c9151-a3fa-5fb0-a971-4ca649a53b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{27}(\\frac{1}{3}) = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578125} {:answer/id #uuid "44b68153-0ddd-5b40-bb1d-32b8f3d68745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{3}}(27) = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578131} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed1895f-1f18-5be3-86bb-2f5dc4487e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{27}(3) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578134} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef644aa-8da7-5fe8-beee-16e7ce983b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{3}}(3) = 27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578135} {:answer/id #uuid "fd207ca7-6cf9-5c7d-b983-c4f8271d8e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3}(27) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ed1895f-1f18-5be3-86bb-2f5dc4487e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{27}(3) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578134} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f4d622a-dc96-5e16-a85d-40eebb97af80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30be9567-aee3-5bee-b633-3520ae651ed4" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{27} = 3$ can be expressed as" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n27^{1/3} = 3\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$\n\nIf we compare $27^{1/3} = 3$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=3$, $b=27$, and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt[3]{27} = 3$ can be expressed as $\\log_{27}(3) = \\frac{1}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598e3024-476f-592b-a65c-78a893183f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(64) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578132} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0b1d81-ae28-54b9-9ac0-676cc08483e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{64}(\\frac{1}{3}) = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578139} {:answer/id #uuid "c558edb6-6010-5848-8e97-cb338f5ea7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(\\frac{1}{3}) = 64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578148} {:answer/id #uuid "d22203ed-bef2-59bf-b0b3-45059df3810a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{3}}(4) = 64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578151} {:answer/id #uuid "d6418f52-8b6a-57be-86da-c9393379eaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{64}(4) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6418f52-8b6a-57be-86da-c9393379eaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{64}(4) = \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578153} :answer-field/id #uuid "1db2c0a8-38b2-5302-b18d-1be9739be07c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "427d942f-df41-51d4-8a8f-35f9b499baf0" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[3]{64} = 4$ can be expressed as" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n64^{1/3} = 4\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$\n\nIf we compare $64^{1/3} = 4$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=4$, $b=64$, and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt[3]{64} = 4$ can be expressed as $\\log_{64}(4) = \\frac{1}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18396296-be09-5ce0-88db-c5e69029bd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{5}(25) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578126} {:answer/id #uuid "80d10372-16d6-5661-81fd-4f97e62312d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{2}}(5) = 25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578141} {:answer/id #uuid "98446917-d3c8-5b22-8707-9c9331f743a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{25}(5) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578143} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd98a92-d424-5cd7-8c90-b8195dcd7048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{25}(\\frac{1}{2}) = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578150} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8377ff-4aa0-52d6-bad4-e8a1b0ffeb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{5}(\\frac{1}{2}) = 25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98446917-d3c8-5b22-8707-9c9331f743a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{25}(5) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578143} :answer-field/id #uuid "926a4fcb-fddd-5767-9608-2950f55a23e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "730885b6-fd17-5299-bd3c-6358d2a12d82" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{25} = 5$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n25^{1/2} = 5\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$\n\nIf we compare $25^{1/2} = 5$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=5$, $b=25$, and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{25} = 5$ is equivalent to $\\log_{25}(5) = \\frac{1}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c522d2-4612-59ce-9ad5-4548baa95ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) = 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578136} {:answer/id #uuid "99a7301f-f67f-5efe-9bab-877899c9168e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{16}(2) = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578144} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ecf327-6d41-5b20-a247-c7a8e56b14f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{4}}(2) = 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578147} {:answer/id #uuid "da32acf1-f672-5aa1-b420-050992f76220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{16}(\\frac{1}{4}) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578154} {:answer/id #uuid "dd564f1f-31f9-5f10-9a1f-6af3cb94a84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}(16) = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a7301f-f67f-5efe-9bab-877899c9168e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{16}(2) = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578144} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5f1479-fa4c-52fa-aae4-519a232a528f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5ae6c39-8693-553b-8e20-55441e09af49" :question/problem "$\\sqrt[4]{16} = 2$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n16^{1/4} = 2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$\n\nIf we compare $16^{1/4} = 2$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=2$, $b=16$, and $x=\\frac{1}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt[4]{16} = 2$ is equivalent to $\\log_{16}(2) = \\frac{1}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "794b1256-0632-5b3a-b63d-f2fb2d41c4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{2}}(2) = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578138} {:answer/id #uuid "abd9f5d2-089e-514d-abfa-09f1186ee2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{2}(4) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578145} {:answer/id #uuid "af57efff-9710-572d-bd32-5adc89ec2b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{\\frac{1}{2}}(4) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578146} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d02272-d636-59f1-b7dc-13ce97b6b349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578149} {:answer/id #uuid "e944426a-20a5-5c44-aa56-3a497c8214b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(2) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e944426a-20a5-5c44-aa56-3a497c8214b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{4}(2) = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578156} :answer-field/id #uuid "29fd7311-bfb6-572d-a1c8-1a566d7f4202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd867eb5-198e-5f11-80c2-685d3d0d3cae" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{4} = 2$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given equation can be written using exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n4^{1/2} = 2\n$$\n\nThe logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$\n\nIf we compare $4^{1/2} = 2$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=2$, $b=4$, and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ is equivalent to $\\log_{4}(2) = \\frac{1}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87c35db5-2008-5dbd-afde-054aac1ff46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578485} {:answer/id #uuid "89528332-60a7-50a6-aae0-62197a6a399c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578488} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd757c7-3b04-5a03-a710-bf666e048b29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578595} {:answer/id #uuid "cea07d9b-f74a-524a-b08f-3e3aadb27338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578596} {:answer/id #uuid "d0657123-7a09-5b4d-b51a-03a6d7445550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87c35db5-2008-5dbd-afde-054aac1ff46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578485} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7fcedc-c163-5f81-978f-4e684776d889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d53e0c0-130a-597e-9eae-4500f595f886" :question/math-academy-id "q-85639" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27074480ce1627c5f5c54596b9adaea1800bcd657a7a392c71be86b27949424.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/574143239dacf6a0eb11307826c62db531384aa03b9c0f4bade4d77c66499c23.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02586119-71be-5aa8-b399-e820d3f3c609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578276} {:answer/id #uuid "9f328edd-5cef-5843-b812-5017a9d8679d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578523} {:answer/id #uuid "a56dc459-8849-5b41-915a-0ec8333886c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578531} {:answer/id #uuid "a899ebb2-c6bf-5cff-b840-d5e06befd72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578540} {:answer/id #uuid "cb513520-510e-5fbc-ae91-42c36c608b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a56dc459-8849-5b41-915a-0ec8333886c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578531} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fb8046-36a6-54cd-bf21-1210cc3f4ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17db8653-72df-5afe-a4b6-7065bd849c60" :question/math-academy-id "q-103144" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "389a5696-c840-56e0-8558-733de5240472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578361} {:answer/id #uuid "4017febd-50d6-5503-af7a-ff948aace873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578378} {:answer/id #uuid "98cc3f2f-f840-5de8-acdb-83f201a4880d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578515} {:answer/id #uuid "aaab6afb-3ab3-5a9f-849c-a2ad0e12fb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578543} {:answer/id #uuid "f37683f8-f240-5221-9235-386d2ba6e331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "389a5696-c840-56e0-8558-733de5240472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578361} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce157c42-a9a4-5ec7-af0b-682ed5561091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20010f38-3e5e-5e3e-a89d-c3c51d40861a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20989" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0bd043f0ff9b6cc575d7912776bd0aaa41dc161b32304115cf6c8dedba86f81a.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52305d0f-2d94-5433-b04b-fbcb2e2da01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578401} {:answer/id #uuid "a4770bae-6ba8-5802-bd0c-756997c15415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578529} {:answer/id #uuid "c2449dff-2ed5-55e8-bb8a-7583e96127a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578573} {:answer/id #uuid "c5dcec46-aaea-508d-b487-98620556bbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578577} {:answer/id #uuid "fd782cf0-d10b-509b-a11e-abbbc730bc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52305d0f-2d94-5433-b04b-fbcb2e2da01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578401} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8549c61-6952-565e-a79d-86243d8c0224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2086b542-4ce0-5886-ad07-2a2bb6e42c3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124968" :question/problem "![](images/df/df379135e9123fe9f0c3a1e03fce4898f153e321c3bd33229f3bd1c68b6249cc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1c/1cb6ef37aaaf228273ee44de5c6fb07a59f4e6f334702b2be1425b5988705855.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{8}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16e9c511-8886-5f56-8ee7-6556baca60f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578309} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef85eba-7368-57c1-8685-8d542b3d4d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578345} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3946a4-4840-5c17-bc49-b6cd5ef2faaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578524} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c0fa7e-1c3b-5c73-8f6c-dd538719b8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578631} {:answer/id #uuid "f30dcc98-6482-51c6-8828-44cf84a10887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c0fa7e-1c3b-5c73-8f6c-dd538719b8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578631} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bfbeb97-b5a7-5edb-811c-c6fd832edf47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21abfc2b-1c65-57da-8fcf-53cc0cb791f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-6840" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=-\\frac{3}{t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{3}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{3}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 0+1} \\\\  & =-\\,3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10346f23-0101-5358-9519-35c89dd53f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578299} {:answer/id #uuid "390c3982-5f08-5df4-9419-1af7d9155284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578362} {:answer/id #uuid "527341ed-fd05-5c3e-a2b4-4b5180a375a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578402} {:answer/id #uuid "d3125639-78db-54c6-9919-bde775ed99dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578601} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0bc71c-fb20-51d7-bf89-57a0e9d0fd4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "390c3982-5f08-5df4-9419-1af7d9155284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb3cc15d-02b4-568e-9ece-4c12ccf4570a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b78c4ee-f2f9-5e8b-ac11-248a23933307" :question/math-academy-id "q-1674" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{2}+1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47721bcb-3d8f-5a12-bcc0-33a4f7d37a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578388} {:answer/id #uuid "60678c6c-34f6-521e-974a-d64948890b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578430} {:answer/id #uuid "92360fb5-afe5-5be2-afa8-7466435ca264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578507} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ad6c0e-f793-5edd-90c4-0ee7b8553e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578541} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e88346-21b3-5083-aad1-974d8b1176d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60678c6c-34f6-521e-974a-d64948890b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578430} :answer-field/id #uuid "23045511-0482-5a00-b7af-8abfa60c5680" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "336f4f03-57a6-57dc-92a1-f56dac8b2445" :question/math-academy-id "q-124833" :question/problem "![](images/97/978383201236edb0e33057b15f0521b4547bac94a7913abba604ef1e176dc8b3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8b/8b37120c20c5b9c6f07337add093d2e582d70f6f5ba2fc1b417b1ed562920173.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2b35f3-d886-5c54-a151-e8d5934eab23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578347} {:answer/id #uuid "60643ae6-c1c1-54cb-b6a5-b7b4b55166dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578429} {:answer/id #uuid "63e5c95a-8586-50f1-b629-8de35c055a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578435} {:answer/id #uuid "8bac3b6b-cbce-58bf-9b70-ee9502dfbc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578493} {:answer/id #uuid "db6a37ce-de59-5b67-9bef-6c00c0d8b028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db6a37ce-de59-5b67-9bef-6c00c0d8b028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578620} :answer-field/id #uuid "0988bf43-8be2-598d-b9b8-92f8fe65a777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37f818fc-6d32-5594-93f9-04ec549a0621" :question/math-academy-id "q-124810" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{25}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{25}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{24}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0938a141-d12b-51e1-9998-a7cc436edc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578288} {:answer/id #uuid "27fa3f4c-12dc-5dbf-874a-1e6639271800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578336} {:answer/id #uuid "45b25f33-bf7f-5def-955e-d497d26f5e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578384} {:answer/id #uuid "47dee097-bdf3-5c60-8d1d-d16d58555770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578389} {:answer/id #uuid "c9028022-51bc-53f4-aee2-72790be92141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47dee097-bdf3-5c60-8d1d-d16d58555770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578389} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ef58acd-fd9a-5753-af67-91bee6f1e34f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661f8f17-e856-564e-ad3f-b602958b65c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11938" :question/problem "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))+(x)(\\frac{1}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{x}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578833 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c722ff-e1b8-5a52-af6d-46d7d47770f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578275} {:answer/id #uuid "638ce517-cf18-5e4a-9cb3-d4f7d60d0039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡3(4-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡3\\operatorname{cot}⁡3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578434} {:answer/id #uuid "771e64c9-e943-52fe-945f-1eea480889b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{csc}^{2}⁡2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578459} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d1c180-4dd3-51bf-b383-ca38bfcc7a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4+3\\operatorname{csc}⁡2\\operatorname{cot}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578555} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c9181f-174f-587d-914d-0780f3a3d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c9181f-174f-587d-914d-0780f3a3d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578658} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ecdc8b-e818-5576-b257-904854acad1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6650c0b4-dbaf-54fa-bdff-16ce8ac264e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-31359" :question/problem "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)$ at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3})(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1))+({x}^{4})(-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)-3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)(4-3x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(1)^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3(1)-1)(4-3(1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(1)-1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17209926-bbeb-5eac-83bc-49f7341ac4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578310} {:answer/id #uuid "7621c886-17c5-53da-ad1b-817216429049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-9,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578458} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4b7c93-b462-5bc0-ba7c-435803992578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578517} {:answer/id #uuid "c1176870-903e-5a26-ab27-2b891f377850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578572} {:answer/id #uuid "da77021a-bceb-532a-a073-05a140340109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da77021a-bceb-532a-a073-05a140340109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578618} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f1f3fe-7cc9-517f-8e81-f601638bcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "696aa9f5-c58d-5b74-b314-032b710e1070" :question/math-academy-id "q-52485" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-4(x+1)(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-1+2}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x+1)(x-2) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{2}+1)(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{1}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9].$\n\n![](images/b4/b45df69ab8780eabce5b57a30cf7448a011361e18e28989249e1eca439b17028.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c712774a-c1bc-5ca0-b374-7906f054c294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sin^2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c712774a-c1bc-5ca0-b374-7906f054c294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sin^2 x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578579} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a3b451a-2a50-5d61-ab5a-17f181f52c94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a7e1449-9e66-5cab-bc6e-62dcc6353f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167318" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-f(x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\cancel{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ad4bc9-fbbe-5928-a45e-a9dc5017529d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578461} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2d373f-505c-5335-8e64-76b8eb295d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578501} {:answer/id #uuid "be71c86d-3b9c-534c-a34b-4b6da4e2d78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578567} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9e04a0-d2bc-5da6-a08f-b95e6df71427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578590} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b50275-efc4-5f5a-9e1f-741741a7800e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be71c86d-3b9c-534c-a34b-4b6da4e2d78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578567} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aaa1f21-66b6-5f2f-a870-f2d386417dc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8613cddf-5858-574b-9d8b-91be11b8ce7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85510" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{7\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{2})=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b36b6ec-2ed2-535b-929e-96d949ec8f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578319} {:answer/id #uuid "85ae0a1c-5407-5808-8ae2-0a514f5e6977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578483} {:answer/id #uuid "ace82a22-a4b8-5727-b37e-68b063a01e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578546} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d97b67-6984-5b57-80bd-aef4f53cf8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578556} {:answer/id #uuid "f9059780-fd11-5593-89b8-51a4b864da31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85ae0a1c-5407-5808-8ae2-0a514f5e6977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578483} :answer-field/id #uuid "163d3cfe-3b9f-5c14-845a-4878ab9e6246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86805245-e211-580c-80b0-1f9e6a9db43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-23470" :question/problem "${sin}^{2}⁡({315}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle ${315}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${315}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{315}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nNow, let's consider the angle ${150}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡({315}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31db1cd7-0388-50c2-b462-7f35cbf65e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31db1cd7-0388-50c2-b462-7f35cbf65e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578351} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f920a3-0d4a-551a-8220-e772169b0aaf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578692} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4f249c-80a1-5293-abb1-c40a523ef755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c4f249c-80a1-5293-abb1-c40a523ef755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578495} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e647b3-cb17-50f5-a019-b6672e2e8687" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "921e549a-bfee-5f3e-b0ea-16a68e62a19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-222090" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-4{x}^{2}-20x-15?$\n\nThe coordinates of the vertex are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ and $b=-20$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-20)}{2(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-20)}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4{x}^{2}-20x-15 \\\\  & =-4{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-20(-\\frac{5}{2})-15 \\\\  & =-25+50-15 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-\\frac{5}{2},10).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02aa900c-d37d-5d89-95d0-ff442793be6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578278} {:answer/id #uuid "761a9162-fdb6-5757-9e66-1974a28d9610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578457} {:answer/id #uuid "85791f91-326d-58bf-b867-2b69dc9dc09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578482} {:answer/id #uuid "93284549-4c49-5b55-b41e-6f6cb9ae87d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578509} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068e8a-8f67-5fdf-a3d7-d7161393d1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93284549-4c49-5b55-b41e-6f6cb9ae87d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578509} :answer-field/id #uuid "995363a3-cf64-5509-8e5f-6562ae68b350" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1057e3e-c482-59e9-af27-f85b622c7e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-23236" :question/problem "If $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, then $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's first find the value of angle $2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n$$\n2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=2(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f8b02c-3c71-509f-93de-e4a3c2bb07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9f8b02c-3c71-509f-93de-e4a3c2bb07cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578646} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9a1810-6a6d-5eb5-883e-8d03107298a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df476431-ab3a-5147-98f8-ef5f1e16d04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-353353" :question/problem "![](images/85/853358cc3207d3f2738ecc3bd2ee6391d62b1a51735c43e16f37a2b83414800e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),\\,y=t{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3}{3t+1},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=(t+1){e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(t+1){e}^{t}}{(\\frac{3}{3t+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)(3t+1){e}^{t}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{(0+1)(0+1){e}^{0}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14008af2-533f-5df0-8073-9df0dea9eabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578302} {:answer/id #uuid "27daefe3-4f08-546a-b49a-e8753adc9acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578334} {:answer/id #uuid "37a43cc4-1a56-5ea6-a470-f77306833ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578359} {:answer/id #uuid "682f8f89-939f-5fdf-acb3-361f9eca56c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578443} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a43da8-b5ab-5aec-b64f-9fe03bede5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14008af2-533f-5df0-8073-9df0dea9eabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578302} :answer-field/id #uuid "1891b75c-a445-57bf-8516-60c344d13f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8ddd7db-0156-5251-bf50-65888b2a7901" :question/math-academy-id "q-103159" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5cbe55-beb8-55d8-b3d7-1b0220ae60dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578370} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddbd52d-d970-5293-adcd-958e5e7719b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578371} {:answer/id #uuid "c3be416c-5de9-5416-9071-8b9539a10c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578576} {:answer/id #uuid "d76ab2b6-88a5-5b3a-a528-1ec41b175396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d5cbe55-beb8-55d8-b3d7-1b0220ae60dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578370} :answer-field/id #uuid "24a1b253-dea2-5722-80f3-4ed1534482dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ab8f72-a107-5264-917d-17e7a0db3bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578386} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2eb7fd-63ac-5bde-a164-58af1a7b608b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578502} {:answer/id #uuid "a65673dc-f1f0-522a-bafe-68ef7278ee0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578535} {:answer/id #uuid "e3410172-077c-5b4c-b1c5-73dcfaebadb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3410172-077c-5b4c-b1c5-73dcfaebadb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578637} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9e19625-804c-5b01-89fe-15a44c087d3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ede3a234-3946-5f25-9a03-9a90ef1ab597" :question/math-academy-id "q-343912" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2})=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A &=\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ 3\\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{8}{27\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=-\\frac{8x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578877 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0825b7a7-74a7-5935-86cd-1b0bba712bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578285} {:answer/id #uuid "5348801e-a631-5d23-93d5-012769dd8d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578403} {:answer/id #uuid "600a5309-7bc1-513f-8449-5d85d2a64fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578428} {:answer/id #uuid "b432d866-9d8e-5893-a7c9-658ab974d99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578554} {:answer/id #uuid "d843b8af-69d9-5879-a337-8dde5a272ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b432d866-9d8e-5893-a7c9-658ab974d99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578554} :answer-field/id #uuid "940ee680-1c54-57c6-9fe1-a296cc68c0ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef5ed0c9-8fa3-5025-804c-fe51ae9e2b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15477" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={sec}^{2}⁡t,\\,y={csc}^{3}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2{sec}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3{csc}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡t{cot}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{3}⁡t})(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}){cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡t}{{sin}^{5}⁡t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cot}^{4}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){cot}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\, \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1211556a-5bf1-58e2-84e7-7950e4319711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578301} {:answer/id #uuid "15a1e317-123e-5b9f-bb99-33f0a7ca6264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578303} {:answer/id #uuid "4f32522d-6848-5f81-b69c-a543a910c74c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578398} {:answer/id #uuid "5eabf2f9-9a99-5893-adc9-74960dc5a0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578425} {:answer/id #uuid "84a5612c-a50c-5501-ad80-806d183376a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a5612c-a50c-5501-ad80-806d183376a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578481} :answer-field/id #uuid "6379ebf1-b8d0-5e84-b58f-943367259fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7944f41-be32-59e2-8b16-d3d6e85a0689" :question/math-academy-id "q-85638" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the third quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{3}{5}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{3}{5}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{9}{25}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the third quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{4}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f72694-f51d-52ef-9284-7e1aa9a2b93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f72694-f51d-52ef-9284-7e1aa9a2b93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579077} :answer-field/id #uuid "294f90db-e9c2-5694-b51f-cd1454cd8c1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a9a6a7-bf2d-5419-bf68-2d0fb932dc93" :question/math-academy-id "q-223674" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(5x+2)(x-6)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=-(5x+2)(x-6),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5}+6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{30}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{28}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{14}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165b5780-9442-5349-8b73-00d7e75626b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579005} {:answer/id #uuid "89a434e7-1e55-544e-813a-7f3db861daed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579206} {:answer/id #uuid "beb1a873-2a94-548e-95d6-bf94cf79b543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579297} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c06aae-4f93-5284-a80a-e1ec60c51441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579382} {:answer/id #uuid "f316960e-822c-5be0-a815-0d51822ea143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f316960e-822c-5be0-a815-0d51822ea143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579383} :answer-field/id #uuid "30beceb0-84c9-5497-96c5-d7d36f453dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c33414b-3ca3-59b7-b0ab-40bd082f7bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-118797" :question/problem "A right triangle has legs of length $\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{3}$ and $\\frac{4}{3}.$ Calculate the length of its hypotenuse." :question/worked-solution "Let $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{4}{3},$ and $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. According to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the values for $a$ and $b$ gives us\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}+\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ad6078-b88f-5380-b29c-8c20cdec4c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579010} {:answer/id #uuid "39f60501-4a58-5ac7-a65e-5cd9504e7474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579078} {:answer/id #uuid "47917957-1abc-575f-a20d-0d4d851c8658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579099} {:answer/id #uuid "65850566-e29c-54a5-bc75-0b0d4fbe9062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579144} {:answer/id #uuid "919dc9ed-cfe4-57c4-98df-5189faade881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47917957-1abc-575f-a20d-0d4d851c8658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579099} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc9adc6-da08-5035-9d61-f7479a64c3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ed53337-f202-58d0-a176-4f7c1b9ce4ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-130899" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy-3{y}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨4,2⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ {4}^{2}+4(2)-3(2)^{2}-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x+y,x-6y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2},{c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ 12 & =4(2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+2({c}_{1}-6{c}_{2}) \\\\ 12 & =10{c}_{1}-8{c}_{2} \\\\ 6 & =5{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{2-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{4-0} \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $5{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}=6,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{c}_{1}-4(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}) & =6 \\\\ 5{c}_{1}-2{c}_{1} & =6 \\\\ 3{c}_{1} & =6 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(2,1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f226985-9e2e-5f56-98d7-bbe1f9ce77e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578988} {:answer/id #uuid "a88f4c6d-b8d1-5a3e-85e1-ee856e58285e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579257} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b2f450-4b43-5f99-befa-31742c8cf3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579273} {:answer/id #uuid "e700c7f0-be9d-5450-8804-46e62b82640e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579351} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2432d4-99f4-52e2-89df-6e374faa1309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd2432d4-99f4-52e2-89df-6e374faa1309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579393} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef90af97-5201-51bf-901a-91196afc1e6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fe3a351-f352-5be0-93b8-6d5db4f32297" :question/math-academy-id "q-123658" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{300}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={60}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡({60}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${60}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4be090-32a2-507f-81d9-31e8f0255492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/49}⁡(\\frac{1}{7})=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579082} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccf37c6-8541-5705-8721-96e5f5f39777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/7}⁡(2)=-49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579105} {:answer/id #uuid "5576f023-1fa4-5dfd-82dc-88103b7025f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{49}⁡(\\frac{1}{7})=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579117} {:answer/id #uuid "abb39a68-2a49-51fd-85db-b217a707f0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/7}⁡(49)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579264} {:answer/id #uuid "dc77b116-61a4-59fb-978e-e2f4f7567c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(49)=\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb39a68-2a49-51fd-85db-b217a707f0da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{1/7}⁡(49)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579264} :answer-field/id #uuid "0096bd54-d4da-5ffb-a10b-6461c6d4ffbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d652a81-ab7a-514a-969a-6e1c713f1003" :question/math-academy-id "q-99456" :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{7})}^{-2}=49$ can be expressed as" :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={b}^{x}$ is equivalent to ${log}_{b}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${(\\frac{1}{7})}^{-2}=49$ with $a={b}^{x},$ we get $a=49,b=\\frac{1}{7}$ and $x=-2.$  \n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${log}_{1/7}⁡(49)=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3458b8e0-6025-506a-9b90-83a87e1eb5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579066} {:answer/id #uuid "87de6df6-a8ce-5658-943f-9010d213853e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579203} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc95180-1e74-5957-a310-ecabcd293f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579216} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a0359b-7017-5065-9517-429d0bfca6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579260} {:answer/id #uuid "cc95fe6d-1797-5643-b52c-84ddbf522487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9a0359b-7017-5065-9517-429d0bfca6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579260} :answer-field/id #uuid "12cd225e-59fc-5a10-ac48-e16436043f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ae3a735-6039-5ae0-a592-be9a89fdd607" :question/math-academy-id "q-84904" :question/problem "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(3x-8)(3x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=-(3x-8)(3x+2),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{8}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{2}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ba1d20-6ac9-5b8f-aa0c-6026cd28f9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579029} {:answer/id #uuid "27a4226b-eed2-512e-8709-2326b4e919ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579035} {:answer/id #uuid "cf807ddf-c4a4-52b4-992d-01a21cbd4939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579319} {:answer/id #uuid "d86269a0-d5b2-5c79-944d-dcac097bca04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579330} {:answer/id #uuid "ef81af81-3f83-5d4e-9475-41bb2afbf175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf807ddf-c4a4-52b4-992d-01a21cbd4939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579319} :answer-field/id #uuid "46ce9f1c-8319-5d3a-a9bd-7b32759cfc72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f0a7cc5-025c-5f3d-b13c-013da7976c41" :question/math-academy-id "q-123673" :question/problem "Find the value of $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡{870}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${870}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{870}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={510}^{∘} \\\\ {510}^{∘}-{320}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{870}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{870}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡{870}^{∘}=6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14455493-8d92-5151-bdab-bc10fadf2eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578999} {:answer/id #uuid "343b75b7-b92f-5a35-b80f-11c7280482ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579063} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd4bb8d-11a5-5f5e-b162-54a41b80f4f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579220} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ba4153-1bfa-573e-8a4b-97009f76fa18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579254} {:answer/id #uuid "ace670a6-afae-5081-80f6-60fefa14d520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7ba4153-1bfa-573e-8a4b-97009f76fa18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579254} :answer-field/id #uuid "72062e97-e13a-500b-b6f7-64a5d2c829e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "947f58ae-d814-594b-bac5-39606db5e45f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89755" :question/problem "Find the value of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${945}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{945}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={585}^{∘} \\\\ {585}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={225}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${225}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cbb2bf4-498c-58c2-be5e-8a696c94568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579047} {:answer/id #uuid "77b1e2de-4d1b-51c4-9e54-d5d762b5713c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579167} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7c6832-4ce6-5c98-99cd-b9fc0727276b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579181} {:answer/id #uuid "85aefb4d-694f-5b72-85a3-d0cafeb15460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579199} {:answer/id #uuid "ea42e0e8-8e6e-50cb-a231-c57815edb359" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77b1e2de-4d1b-51c4-9e54-d5d762b5713c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579167} :answer-field/id #uuid "704db9fd-b847-51df-8430-8607508a4e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bce6c5a-0643-548e-b425-54109cb350ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-42332" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(3,-3\\sqrt{3}).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+27} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{(-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\sqrt{3}| \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(6,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$\n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 6 & =k\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 6 & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69d814c075c7e8a940fe4900f5601052835ab999e68e43fabe1dd34eaf5709b2.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f78c05-bf0a-564f-a669-56c7dde661b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579191} {:answer/id #uuid "846eaa4a-b893-50bf-b64a-39f82c5b7476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579196} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd97aaa-ac61-5cf9-8140-7652339fca14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579291} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd233c2-8e00-5e0e-8f30-b9a97c76eb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579315} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a72048-9242-52fe-8157-6a66f8292266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f78c05-bf0a-564f-a669-56c7dde661b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579191} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2dd57d1-0385-516f-896a-e6e6cc8fffe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c60ffe9-712c-51e1-a883-3ef22c16bc57" :question/math-academy-id "q-1691" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{300}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{300}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={60}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${60}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba003d7-6822-5351-9fe7-349e06fc8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579044} {:answer/id #uuid "4c491272-cad8-5a95-9ff9-8d544bca7e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579103} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc326e4-39aa-59d0-9995-1c21de4103c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579215} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d46ad2-0466-5bcb-b853-ea0fc19ba251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579250} {:answer/id #uuid "f21cb5bf-3232-5255-a94f-7f607f76cb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f21cb5bf-3232-5255-a94f-7f607f76cb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579378} :answer-field/id #uuid "949a1db0-2e58-54bd-8e7a-b416759e3f8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4e2b7c0-aa7f-5e60-b7cc-99adc6546180" :question/math-academy-id "q-64924" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=-\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c87004-23ba-569a-9c9b-b89eeccfb56d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579024} {:answer/id #uuid "42121e6e-9031-571f-8fc2-a00e89928f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579089} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f40621-37d9-5f8d-9408-bcc23b9c495d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579277} {:answer/id #uuid "f6569e0e-bfee-5cd3-8a9a-d1d7a6c6953a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579387} {:answer/id #uuid "f8741f6f-93ed-597b-88dd-a0aa8ad5a364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8741f6f-93ed-597b-88dd-a0aa8ad5a364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579388} :answer-field/id #uuid "85afbf53-a50a-529d-b696-6bb998e797a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee81d71b-117d-5b90-9487-69a61ec1a15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64942" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186579576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06c524c-97c9-571d-887e-2ec12c21b024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579244} {:answer/id #uuid "bea5dc77-ebd1-5a6b-b874-2871bcab41e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579295} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b0e014-e415-57b4-bd55-9cb7c771d508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579328} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe42df2-c1cd-5100-a74d-8965f74b92d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579334} {:answer/id #uuid "ef30e1c5-71ec-5346-81a5-60e9cafefea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef30e1c5-71ec-5346-81a5-60e9cafefea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579369} :answer-field/id #uuid "54f20341-5524-513a-9b1a-88215ae1d662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdd7cf3f-8536-5ff0-aafa-89d7ca6fc326" :question/math-academy-id "q-37931" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)]}_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi +y))\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi +y))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +y)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi +0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ae2a2-57f7-5488-af75-79fb59c74df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579677} {:answer/id #uuid "15516f3a-c0ed-5bbd-a792-fe9f912b1913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579714} {:answer/id #uuid "a65dbf10-14e5-5728-a726-d6b72f27f37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579960} {:answer/id #uuid "db52c63a-c0e1-5e36-b3a2-1810317849d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580051} {:answer/id #uuid "db6401c8-086a-509a-a29a-23ad1c9142f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}+2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db52c63a-c0e1-5e36-b3a2-1810317849d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580051} :answer-field/id #uuid "eef8c0f8-5bf3-5ab4-88d2-624213e64652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20e2fb06-75fc-5406-b1dc-e81b5249a3bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-103201" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ then $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-6$ into the left-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-12\\pi )=6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})=-2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3227c650-6c66-5a55-9504-c55173bdeabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579763} {:answer/id #uuid "399e497e-7edf-5c8b-bb71-12243da891a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579776} {:answer/id #uuid "410d14d8-629c-5258-95ff-63054dd04390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579793} {:answer/id #uuid "640244e9-5910-5ff4-93e8-90b28ad3fa1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579841} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6daca6-1a59-52f3-af4c-eca50384843a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399e497e-7edf-5c8b-bb71-12243da891a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579776} :answer-field/id #uuid "a400fe05-b0fe-5f9a-a033-2cc157672ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31d526c9-cd4d-5afd-b547-b21b29aa4a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-73558" :question/problem "${\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}({5}^{y-3}\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}({5}^{y-3}\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}[{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}{[\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{x})}^{3}]}_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}{5}^{y-3}(\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{4})}^{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{(\\sqrt{0})}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{16\\cdot {5}^{y-3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{16\\cdot {5}^{y-3}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{16}{3}{[\\frac{{5}^{y-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{{5}^{4-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{5}^{3-3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{5-1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d033f63-1d66-5ce2-bcb1-637c021ea285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579696} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4328c5-5492-5329-9034-e97e26c1a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579754} {:answer/id #uuid "464e29c0-4ca0-5be3-aa58-9dd2759740f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579801} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc6b00c-4eaf-56a8-867d-290428433023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579814} {:answer/id #uuid "d159542f-d8cb-5fd5-a9b0-7f44e7e3e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc6b00c-4eaf-56a8-867d-290428433023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8abad105-d627-54a6-90a0-f416d5cd11ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53b06cb2-e2f8-59e9-806d-b2e5cf57453e" :question/math-academy-id "q-113749" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =2{x}^{3}+4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2{x}^{3}+4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 6 & 20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{6}^{20}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{6}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{20}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edd0587-b80c-5a5d-b074-82d25d6a73de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579700} {:answer/id #uuid "757341cc-68c9-5023-95d1-db7937d02e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579869} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd0479-6fc9-5117-a735-fefbc3024eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579880} {:answer/id #uuid "97af7509-7658-573b-b435-5060c5a50991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579931} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4618cb-d781-50ed-992b-dfa48edfd64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757341cc-68c9-5023-95d1-db7937d02e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579869} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f7bbfd-ac9d-5ccd-abdb-e44df258f098" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64a73ec8-a2f2-57ef-9a04-50abdd49fead" :question/math-academy-id "q-103208" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1=$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1 &=-10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})+1=2\\sqrt{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2121c2f9-54ec-5628-b7cc-ae06f7d0ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579736} {:answer/id #uuid "534d4abb-6da7-5ef7-8ba7-4e7aeb98d90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579820} {:answer/id #uuid "6dff903f-e185-584b-8b74-2e2a864cccbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579858} {:answer/id #uuid "78d516f5-dbe2-52c8-aa16-48cd5d4e15fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579879} {:answer/id #uuid "c89d9057-063c-56b1-99b9-dee392a92ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2121c2f9-54ec-5628-b7cc-ae06f7d0ec10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579736} :answer-field/id #uuid "e741ec4d-b33f-59f0-952c-d99444a295ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ea78e8a-2d26-5cb2-90ce-10f5dc605094" :question/math-academy-id "q-136542" :question/problem "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡a \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101503d8-ac1e-519c-b643-3145ef500675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579702} {:answer/id #uuid "34532bb4-3b6e-51fa-b8f4-8f1df6ae53b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579768} {:answer/id #uuid "6d52c566-ed29-5bd3-b9c4-4764a5933e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} {:answer/id #uuid "d064234d-5324-5917-96ae-62821d5c1cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580026} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cfb4b2-5304-54e2-9284-c03e92de6812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d52c566-ed29-5bd3-b9c4-4764a5933e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579857} :answer-field/id #uuid "f95b7983-f575-5576-8124-46c508261fb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90429821-063a-5b87-91d0-cc785ad38d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126175" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2) & =P(X\\le 2)-P(X\\le \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =F(2)-F(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca860ca-1bce-5c95-bf20-df60d5073a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579942} {:answer/id #uuid "ba27bc13-aa91-5810-af6d-8927013670e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579986} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6f8c5c-a483-504f-bde0-740e5465f479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580021} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc9183-fffa-5efb-b96a-bba2f1ebfef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580056} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c9a237-8761-5b58-a458-0ac3e2d56369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba27bc13-aa91-5810-af6d-8927013670e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579986} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbf5b5a-e886-5680-94b8-ec7a52bf455e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4ada4a1-8379-5844-873d-7530ca6e23ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-102371" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{g(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x))}^{′}}{(g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{′}(x))}^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{g}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7c3bba-a4de-5381-8288-8b3a3666d70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725098acf4bef4d9d34f45e8dcf3d44bca342cb4490380a5de6993778bcec0cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579731} {:answer/id #uuid "3213cd65-1687-527e-b318-aa93d61e04f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/174432d35b30691b54762c41be2e5587e64eee45c36a235622cf7d3c6d69357e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579762} {:answer/id #uuid "64a26e6d-e6ea-566b-bb6e-208885d72cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f469e6a205f530e9b98ee3ef061cb7f938e86d214f1a3ed41d5b963ef9bab4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579843} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5bc494-b70c-566f-8e3e-3820e7c02d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70d802eaac422bed7450db74e99cae4598fe27c498b6d75f5ff858513cc5a4e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579972} {:answer/id #uuid "d05a986c-cf21-501f-a198-58ddfa0888d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4b64bf094402621345fbca574c29d9ffe2dbc6de7d6c95c90d37898aab3b006a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05a986c-cf21-501f-a198-58ddfa0888d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4b64bf094402621345fbca574c29d9ffe2dbc6de7d6c95c90d37898aab3b006a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580025} :answer-field/id #uuid "712048fa-195a-5653-b798-8a02ed257182" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bcd6b9ef-34af-5d07-a2d1-6c14934637fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70292" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot 5=5\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $5\\pi ,$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec77ab8cf1ed3d66c0715abbe4f803d41cb876363307bc3f4e27394c0161053d.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39421a8e-415f-5a66-b400-40a2817f0153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39421a8e-415f-5a66-b400-40a2817f0153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579775} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ff9c1d-3810-5604-a0fe-70128423e48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c74e516b-0f8d-5d67-a6fa-3383a90be5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-262452" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e38b72a-de59-5cd2-876c-41cdfae845ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9af8a23313cbcfea757befb3a135869fb724b44af4d7d3e3ee9d5cd5db821453.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579698} {:answer/id #uuid "1a612360-627a-593c-bdb0-763542c7b05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcccc090807ef73b87b4fe2f8950ab057bf484c85a27cec55c04108f58fb4438.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579722} {:answer/id #uuid "327fdac1-d5e9-5ac7-9c96-644217dc4cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4eff259ad6aaf200a274b1fb0972b4850837ffa1fd40ebf517f0c4f77956ec5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579764} {:answer/id #uuid "3521a9e2-c8f7-518c-83f0-67697f00841f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/270e32d36fbc20004ff91af1f73fad320af720f81b9e7c092f335e22a4f7e8b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579769} {:answer/id #uuid "601ef556-5aab-56d8-a151-b02e7ed977a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21e32cb520b6057503db999ec156399680480a27f04aa50290b56818483766cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a612360-627a-593c-bdb0-763542c7b05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bcccc090807ef73b87b4fe2f8950ab057bf484c85a27cec55c04108f58fb4438.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579722} :answer-field/id #uuid "0115aeb9-8988-53ae-aa65-c5fb88987f84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc96eed1-562d-564e-989f-42cdc2296582" :question/math-academy-id "q-72759" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/189aa1ea4e33d47bd26027af4990020c837211a58d53668e25f9210353faa784.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c93837-d2f5-58bc-a841-935f7b08b658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579685} {:answer/id #uuid "1e680472-4d5f-554e-a921-972ebea0b7f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579730} {:answer/id #uuid "4103d277-f890-5702-906a-f19c16ce9abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579792} {:answer/id #uuid "71b5c202-b054-5832-a969-d7b23f696a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579865} {:answer/id #uuid "7e73025a-f67c-522e-baa4-aa14f1358ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4103d277-f890-5702-906a-f19c16ce9abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d6eb5a-a310-5061-85c8-964416a21fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9752437-70b8-5a90-968d-cf807ea578b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72047" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y]_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fb603f-99e1-55bc-9588-77e136b79039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580450} {:answer/id #uuid "492a6a5d-3a6f-5f71-b86d-8254bca584d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5566" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580510} {:answer/id #uuid "5403b4f9-e3bd-50a9-94a2-ee5fe8970f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0906" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580527} {:answer/id #uuid "6b537724-55d3-50f0-841c-0c2a4b421cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580568} {:answer/id #uuid "9661636a-57b9-51c0-bed2-cb7d7f17f0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9661636a-57b9-51c0-bed2-cb7d7f17f0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0656" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580640} :answer-field/id #uuid "4029d8d3-196b-5f66-b128-4c9c3eb421c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "052845df-31c1-5ac6-8fdb-db6eae2b480b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108283" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈6.30168\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 6.30168 \\\\  & ≈0.0656\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114cd42d-ba52-5869-9448-93a17d0920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580410} {:answer/id #uuid "4edb41e5-8763-5f56-92a0-923aac665da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580521} {:answer/id #uuid "921a2907-c9c2-55f3-a5c1-c088eea2d222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580634} {:answer/id #uuid "d55a31f3-9f9c-5cf2-9b86-12761d10e402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580719} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7d360d-043f-5158-a0ad-2486d0c89e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4edb41e5-8763-5f56-92a0-923aac665da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580521} :answer-field/id #uuid "1acf62d4-21d8-564e-9008-9e94eb8fcbe4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d4c2c34-0e64-5420-b51f-0cd0b469fb94" :question/math-academy-id "q-13524" :question/problem "Calculate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3b08c5-67ec-556a-9db8-346afb23990d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3b08c5-67ec-556a-9db8-346afb23990d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580406} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e9c3ac-e070-5fba-b495-8a7967767527" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ccd5c9d-8efc-5890-b6e0-7b6adea1884d" :question/math-academy-id "q-350055" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /12$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /4$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =2(2-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3138bb-1417-57f0-b00e-e24da7b370ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f3138bb-1417-57f0-b00e-e24da7b370ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580434} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f0429a-8ccc-52fa-a654-3ae343603cc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2018f257-8b60-511d-90d3-af91acbd86bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-350039" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c31c8f6-d9d8-53c5-b2d5-4515c416102e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580428} {:answer/id #uuid "5de5a8c0-40fe-5080-8dcd-16fa4bea9378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580544} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9a5de0-f659-5f3b-acd0-b7b08a36484f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580628} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c414a5-ad71-5780-8d90-e3844295abee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580715} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6e8ea5-4e2c-5195-ac1d-c3213d882366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de5a8c0-40fe-5080-8dcd-16fa4bea9378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580544} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e64c80-fb76-53ed-a478-ee475bda1a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26a6ef81-4a35-59c2-9081-35e0aed550d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49545" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/16)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/16}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/4)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{(x/4)}^{2}}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580897 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8233fce-5fa1-504d-a858-b5359a37944d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8233fce-5fa1-504d-a858-b5359a37944d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580778} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d8f522-565f-5640-81d7-21d42b1024a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ed18e1b-5d9e-5d88-b764-bf87259e7a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-350908" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ee8018b-4c2c-5969-bab1-98bc54c84ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580546} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ca949-563a-5a3e-845e-2d7ec457bf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580566} {:answer/id #uuid "7494aed9-29fc-5421-9cd6-437ef544113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580590} {:answer/id #uuid "82b2c57d-6347-5d32-9f6a-25b6b00af7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580610} {:answer/id #uuid "de4fd513-ada9-5107-a3f8-6bff6ac94e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6ca949-563a-5a3e-845e-2d7ec457bf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580566} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf91fbd-c400-5c7a-a3c0-a17b20ba8a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "48ce0cc1-da3f-5716-9932-2b23b9354edd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117619" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca9f4065965fc7d50c0c19087172fbb7591d573bdc571bd1477737eaef8161ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡Bx+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. Given that $B>0,$ what is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-3\\pi )=4\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the period, we get\n\n$$\n4\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{2\\pi }{4\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nComparing the graphs of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ and the above, we see that the above graph is a vertical translation of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $3$ units up. \n\n![](images/2f/2f898960995c28b1a084dd6a19afdbc275259c411350de62bcaf6061df3f2240.png)\n\nSo, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+3.\n$$\n\nFinally, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+3=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9862e91c-0efe-5bb8-8925-45bc31839846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9862e91c-0efe-5bb8-8925-45bc31839846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580645} :answer-field/id #uuid "65d0530e-816a-5976-ad52-1c7704a78646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49d4a023-e567-5ef0-bff2-0a05874f4d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-350892" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157ba3a6-4cca-5941-a534-c9c20fa09bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580420} {:answer/id #uuid "3949c7e0-bdb2-5737-8510-f0d11b3691e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580482} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef527f8-4905-5cfc-a055-ae7479e16a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580490} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d38d57-8f2d-5651-9702-d6a449719bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580664} {:answer/id #uuid "c919e599-9d99-561f-836d-c7fe18e1e60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ef527f8-4905-5cfc-a055-ae7479e16a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580490} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d8cdce-d6e8-500b-b341-549a9fb54bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cef5015-939e-5574-8406-e8591217fe5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-117641" :question/problem "![](images/68/68cc2e6c02238663e248316b63a462c32a5225842728c563adc738cdcb42e417.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/56/56b1b17826611d0a0b63f24342094d0ef99e9c6c8428829b4a2bdd3593e0caa7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally,  stretch it parallel to the $x$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23bed5a7-946a-5761-b3c6-c169f961d99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{4}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580446} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3f4fae-e3f0-5ab1-aeab-69c1e6b34bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580458} {:answer/id #uuid "4d845e5f-48d1-56a8-a9a9-92289951f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} {:answer/id #uuid "7895e320-c61b-57ba-84d3-b9a150bd1eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580593} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7089bb-a038-5d2b-a34b-9bf3c599cdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d845e5f-48d1-56a8-a9a9-92289951f110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580518} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd737fdb-a1db-508c-9c67-7a25aa8d3a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51526ec7-ea58-5f68-9175-85663bcbb887" :question/math-academy-id "q-108284" :question/problem "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<3)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f7f8af-dd67-585c-8b4c-22006bfdb024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02f7f8af-dd67-585c-8b4c-22006bfdb024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580389} :answer-field/id #uuid "3805d3f0-30ff-5610-aef3-e77ea0b75940" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6196cc63-e579-5b66-890d-b1daabdef7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-350036" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{2\\pi x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\pi }(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45daf244-4c3f-5472-93a8-da7d6c3bdccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{16})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580505} {:answer/id #uuid "52196bc9-8d38-5c9d-83d2-2c96f288c9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580525} {:answer/id #uuid "b5431721-a52a-5556-b365-52a555669b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580682} {:answer/id #uuid "d171db58-409b-5e77-8fc1-6b70a1ac11c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3853a5f-a3bd-5d18-8620-4554383b00ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5431721-a52a-5556-b365-52a555669b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580682} :answer-field/id #uuid "1444f051-3ccb-51c7-99bf-4f860a1031ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86968101-fa3c-5286-a648-9b995fa5fe70" :question/math-academy-id "q-114003" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+1)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u={cos}^{3}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $9/8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{9/8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{(\\frac{9}{8})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d020a63-ff8a-5103-950e-32a99925bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d020a63-ff8a-5103-950e-32a99925bad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580596} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a470524-f944-5b81-ad61-34953fcf821f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "891837cc-fc66-582c-9fc5-1ebbcc953137" :question/math-academy-id "q-350053" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /9}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $\\pi /18$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /9}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}3{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23957abc-dcc1-5b7b-b7fd-001f7457d167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580445} {:answer/id #uuid "982579e3-83e0-5545-ad26-b221c0744545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{5})+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580644} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd6becb-70ef-5fc9-86e0-64d33c238ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580709} {:answer/id #uuid "d79deab6-1af2-5703-a987-c1a179e4bf66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580725} {:answer/id #uuid "f00b9b0f-679b-51f4-a9d8-05b26e14d8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f00b9b0f-679b-51f4-a9d8-05b26e14d8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580767} :answer-field/id #uuid "192e842c-aa36-501f-8fbd-126807e8ffc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9198c09e-55b1-58ef-a675-b557b8606f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117650" :question/problem "![](images/67/67e90ea05c5461f3e569fcced387784d8a2e3c3779976825324137d94d85bad6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/20/2090e695566d64097143c31d83e17ea965cd899a2ed3e91536242f84bf538961.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $x$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}).$\n\n- Finally, shift it **up** by $3$ units to give $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eba5ea3-c760-543a-9428-7f559f0c5c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580462} {:answer/id #uuid "35bf6bf5-de1e-5a2d-bdb4-baab3ce1b96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580475} {:answer/id #uuid "7eeaf67a-e2d5-5432-a061-9f5572d2c070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580601} {:answer/id #uuid "9c486bb7-eca1-5a11-b1ef-4e49680dd755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/