olution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/07/07415c592730ede309f0ba0979d203337e4cfcf2ebfa3b7215a27cedf7f6673f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units **left.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a0e0ac-e727-5461-b976-19c124b8b464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580388} {:answer/id #uuid "4890fcde-2797-5117-8088-b582d7722617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580508} {:answer/id #uuid "6d616f73-2fa0-5847-8b9f-9a01caad223b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580574} {:answer/id #uuid "cb597cd5-c5ef-508d-a0c1-618aa29883dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580705} {:answer/id #uuid "e36ac8bb-58e1-59ed-a6aa-fe6e8c50369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb597cd5-c5ef-508d-a0c1-618aa29883dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580705} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e41e02-7a09-5398-bc9b-a3b506f57d51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9cfe1a63-f205-5796-abd7-66f03dc0449c" :question/math-academy-id "q-13655" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3041c79b-6e78-5fa3-b00e-6bdf88d26a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580467} {:answer/id #uuid "39ef5c6b-d0ab-5cba-92ac-955caaab795b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580485} {:answer/id #uuid "91c2deee-4294-5cbc-abc1-cc4197e05f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580633} {:answer/id #uuid "9afd962e-7297-5ee9-bb39-09ac0280e0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580648} {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ae82-1421-5b26-baf9-b60ca1cd7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd44ae82-1421-5b26-baf9-b60ca1cd7fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580787} :answer-field/id #uuid "d376e0d4-0d69-5658-b561-f7eed6420bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f249aac-7cbc-5db4-94cc-8029f3c1555f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117133" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5be745626347b7056d054a221f05353b0da19b09093389be55b70ff6d1131083.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/d4/d454fce5ce753dada69e9c927279237760cb32434cb4743bad04c62f10798cc9.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $\\frac{3}{4}$ unit **right.**\n\nTherefore, the given graph is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{3}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151e86b6-8350-5aa4-a3a6-fbb354525ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580416} {:answer/id #uuid "153031d5-06ee-5864-aaf1-7e3797acbcaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580418} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0207e2-b065-568a-bf91-9f6dd9a3a7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580539} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c99af-debf-5fad-8201-b22a60857246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580649} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1ef8f4-a34d-5fe2-9ed4-6536a59930aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "151e86b6-8350-5aa4-a3a6-fbb354525ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580416} :answer-field/id #uuid "b84f9b78-c8a9-5865-af16-52c04753c6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b10e9e5c-0af8-57a2-8504-99b776b739fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65643" :question/problem "![](images/10/10676bab3ebb60268879642c837f68e55997d4c649d010addb6e4d64ad7ed1bb.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4}),$ where $A$ is a positive constant. What is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The constant $A$ is the amplitude of the function. \n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is ${y}_{max}=10$ and the minimum value is ${y}_{min}=-10.$ Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2}=\\frac{10-(-10)}{2}=\\frac{20}{2}=10.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f6ec7-2c26-556a-b275-e470f79fe0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580398} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8770f4-ae4b-5603-ac25-45beafa1ecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580409} {:answer/id #uuid "2991ad70-06b1-5108-bde4-df2572be3338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580454} {:answer/id #uuid "43221ccd-9516-5e86-b6d5-e44a6d2ae848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580499} {:answer/id #uuid "b42bc523-d2c6-590a-bad6-1c58f9a14c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e8770f4-ae4b-5603-ac25-45beafa1ecd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580409} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcd09329-146d-5461-81c4-c8ae03d7acd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b618bbba-0151-5ccc-837c-c0d410af93f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76235" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:Z\\to Z$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n\nIII. $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=-2x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not*  a surjection.  There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $\\in R.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Z.$ There is no number $x\\in Z$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Z.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in Q,$ choose $x=-\\frac{y}{2}\\in Q.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\frac{y}{2})=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{2})=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a38d2-5ac3-59e4-b944-d3d0d9cc05e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580457} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580522} {:answer/id #uuid "60405895-e035-58cb-842d-10b09a8a4932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580549} {:answer/id #uuid "bf692bc8-d65d-5ceb-9734-cda57421cb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580691} {:answer/id #uuid "dab99a72-47a9-51d7-b3b5-d9c4ea5ed620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fb882bb-be71-5c59-a634-65b02781c9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580522} :answer-field/id #uuid "016466b0-e060-5aab-b591-d71625582e46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c579c1f3-be8e-5aac-8600-10eab58ffbc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49549" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e040bd-d2d8-5b84-97d3-e62216ecff52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580452} {:answer/id #uuid "36b7e2f4-59b9-5bdf-9c4a-7c0ca5ef3c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580478} {:answer/id #uuid "92f3b206-d8a5-5493-bb6a-dd1eaa00549e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580636} {:answer/id #uuid "a6065baa-6d98-5e0c-9f9f-ea438af26e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580665} {:answer/id #uuid "ee423b76-2fdd-5613-b424-50b7839fb2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b7e2f4-59b9-5bdf-9c4a-7c0ca5ef3c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580478} :answer-field/id #uuid "629a59e6-4934-5c6a-88e2-14351298e9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7ee7f5e-2027-5516-b089-c04ab6a54516" :question/math-academy-id "q-6506" :question/problem "![](images/53/5369c10e92ebfcb584885b4f5622dcbb817b6d7d51869cefe0fddcf76d372530.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's add the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ to the plot.\n\n![](images/da/da921ea4084bc5568d744a1394e6021de70ed9884aadd0fa1a3d9a2f65f5303b.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a07989-d7b7-5dff-b703-3bfdca7714a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580385} {:answer/id #uuid "045c6482-91c7-5d6e-9772-0f006eaf5d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580392} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5faef1-427d-52c7-a142-952b50f145d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580404} {:answer/id #uuid "4c53b5b1-1213-5925-8366-669db02e6039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580515} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff7e09d-c627-5462-9c56-8b370684b47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff7e09d-c627-5462-9c56-8b370684b47d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580602} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c2e4c9-1b1b-52f5-a6fb-ffa253311b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186580837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0a45f95-dc7e-5123-8ee5-1bed639f2d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-117037" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcf5bd62bbcaee664ceb36404d3d31d42e726e52c250141a12a9c841563ce039.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the maximum value is ${y}_{max}=\\frac{5}{2}$ and the minimum value is ${y}_{min}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Amplitude } & =\\frac{{y}_{max}-{y}_{min}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2}-(-\\frac{5}{2}))}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
